@MISC{Musto_undated-ao,
  title        = "Fairness and popularity bias in recommender systems: An
                  empirical evaluation",
  author       = "Musto, Cataldo and Lops, Pasquale and Semeraro, Giovanni and
                  Mascari, Viviana and Palmonari, Matteo and Vizzari, Giuseppe",
  abstract     = "In this paper, we present the results of an empirical
                  evaluation investigating how recommendation algorithms are
                  affected by popularity bias. Popularity bias makes more
                  popular items to be recommended more frequently than less
                  popular ones, thus it is one of the most relevant issues that
                  limits the fairness of recommender systems. In particular, we
                  define an experimental protocol based on two state-of-theart
                  datasets containing users' preferences on movies and books and
                  three different recommendation paradigms, i.e., collaborative
                  filtering, content-based filtering and graph-based algorithms.
                  In order to evaluate the overall fairness of the
                  recommendations we use well-known metrics such as Catalogue
                  Coverage, Gini Index and Group Average Popularity (ΔGAP). The
                  goal of this paper is: (i) to provide a clear picture of how
